{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The only obligation in `requirements.cli.clap` is that the `habeas` CLI uses `clap` to define and parse its commands and options. A test can only observe this through clap's behavior.\n\n`tests/cli.rs#fn[clap_defines_commands_options_and_usage_errors]` checks the following:\n- `--help` succeeds and prints clap-style `Usage: habeas <COMMAND>`.\n- The help lists the commands mdbook, setup, status, info, author and help, and leaves out the hidden `hook` command.\n- `status --help` shows the `--all` and `--verbose` options.\n- The `author-evidence` alias parses to the author command.\n- Three usage-error cases exit with code 2 and print clap's messages plus a `Usage: habeas` line: an unknown argument, a missing required argument, and conflicting options.\n\n`tests/coverage.rs#fn[obsolete_status_coverage_flag_is_rejected_with_usage]` adds a clap rejection of an unknown `status --coverage` flag. It asserts exit code 2, the \"unexpected argument\" message and `Usage:`.\n\nTogether these tests cover command definition, option definition, parsing and parse-error behavior. `src/main.rs` also shows that `fn[main]` calls `Cli::parse()` on a clap `Parser`/`Subcommand` derive. The tests give no reason to doubt that, so I found no gap.",
  "references": "57944706d23d6efa1af9d3206f355946eefcff23d1231a08d1fe1ddcfc107fbb",
